  <p>If you'd rather hand the whole job over, <a href="/services/design/short-video">the short video service</a> is the studio cutting the same recording to a brief. <a href="/solutions/platforms/tiktok">The TikTok platform page</a> covers what the studio makes for TikTok, and <a href="/resources/how-to">the how-to guides</a> carry the other workflows.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <h3>How long can a YouTube Short be?</h3>

  <p>Up to three minutes. YouTube treats a square or vertical video of three minutes or less, uploaded on or after October 15, 2024, as a Short. One catch: YouTube's English help page says a Short over one minute with an active Content ID claim is blocked everywhere; the French page says new ones under three minutes are no longer blocked automatically from September 24, 2026. Either way, a longer Short needs music you own or no music at all.</p>

  <h3>How long can a TikTok be when an app posts it?</h3>

  <p>TikTok's developer documentation says every creator can post 3-minute videos through an app, and some accounts can post 5 or 10 minutes. hubStudio publishing to TikTok is in Beta and takes clips of 3 seconds to 10 minutes; when a clip runs longer than your account allows, the publishing step tells you.</p>

  <h3>How long can an Instagram Reel be?</h3>

  <p>Instagram lets reels add up to 20 minutes, but says reels over 3 minutes are not recommended to new audiences. For clips cut from a long video, that makes three minutes the practical ceiling. Through hubStudio, a Reel can run from 3 seconds to 15 minutes, posted on a professional account.</p>

  <h3>Can I post the same clip on all three platforms?</h3>

  <p>Yes. All three take a 9:16 file, and Shorts autopilot saves one MP4 at 1080 by 1920 that fits each. Keep it under a minute if its music could draw a Content ID claim, and give each network its own caption: the autopilot writes one per network you select. Upload the clean file everywhere, never a copy saved back from another app.</p>

  <h3>Do Shorts and Reels need burned-in captions?</h3>

  <p>TikTok's own creative guidance recommends captions or text overlays. YouTube can add automatic captions, but viewers switch them on and YouTube warns they can misread speech. Captions burned into the picture are the ones you checked and every viewer sees. Shorts autopilot burns them in, timed word by word, in one of five looks.</p>

  <h3>Can I put music on a short?</h3>

  <p>Yes, in the Video editor's Sound panel, from your computer or the library. Use only music you have the rights to. TikTok and Instagram mute or block a video whose music they don't license, and on YouTube a Content ID claim can block a Short over one minute or cost it its monetization.</p>

  <h3>Does hubStudio post my Shorts to YouTube?</h3>

  <p>No. YouTube keeps uploads from apps it hasn't audited private, so hubStudio prepares the post instead: the video, a title and a description written to YouTube's rules. Publish interactively copies the title, opens YouTube Studio and downloads the video, and you publish or schedule it there.</p>
